---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Privacy Policy - Pascal Krason" description="Privacy policy and information on data processing">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 pt-32">
    <a 
      href="/" 
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-stone-500 hover:text-orange-600 mb-8 transition-colors min-h-[44px]"
    >
      <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
      </svg>
      Back to Home
    </a>
    
    <header class="mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-stone-800 mb-4">
        Privacy Policy
      </h1>
      <p class="text-stone-600 text-lg">
        Last updated: October 2026
      </p>
    </header>
    
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">1. Privacy at a Glance</h2>
        
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">General Information</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          The following information provides a simple overview of what happens to your personal data when you visit this website. Personal data is any data with which you can be personally identified. Detailed information on the subject of data protection can be found in our privacy policy below this text.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Data Collection on This Website</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          <strong>Who is responsible for data collection on this website?</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Data processing on this website is carried out by the website operator. You can find their contact details in the section "Information on the Responsible Party" in this privacy policy.
        </p>

        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          <strong>How do we collect your data?</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          On the one hand, your data is collected by you providing it to us. This can be, for example, data that you enter in a contact form. Other data is collected automatically or after your consent when visiting the website by our IT systems. This is mainly technical data (e.g., internet browser, operating system, or time of page access). This data is collected automatically as soon as you enter this website.
        </p>

        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          <strong>What do we use your data for?</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Some of the data is collected to ensure error-free provision of the website. Other data may be used to analyze your user behavior.
        </p>

        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          <strong>What rights do you have regarding your data?</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          You have the right to receive information free of charge at any time about the origin, recipient, and purpose of your stored personal data. You also have a right to request the correction or deletion of this data. If you have given consent to data processing, you can revoke this consent at any time for the future. You also have the right to request the restriction of the processing of your personal data under certain circumstances. Furthermore, you have the right to lodge a complaint with the competent supervisory authority.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          You can contact us at any time regarding this and other questions on the subject of data protection.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">2. Hosting</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          This website is hosted on GitHub Pages. The provider is GitHub, Inc., 88 Colin P Kelly Jr St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA (hereinafter "GitHub").
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          When you visit this website, GitHub collects various log files including your IP addresses. For details, please refer to GitHub's privacy policy: <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 hover:text-orange-700 underline">https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement</a>
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          The use of GitHub Pages is based on Art. 6 para. 1 lit. f GDPR. We have a legitimate interest in the most reliable presentation of our website. If a corresponding consent has been requested, processing is carried out exclusively on the basis of Art. 6 para. 1 lit. a GDPR and § 25 para. 1 TTDSG, insofar as the consent includes the storage of cookies or access to information on the user's end device (e.g., device fingerprinting) within the meaning of the TTDSG. The consent can be revoked at any time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">3. General Information and Mandatory Information</h2>
        
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Data Protection</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          The operators of these pages take the protection of your personal data very seriously. We treat your personal data confidentially and in accordance with the statutory data protection regulations and this privacy policy.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          When you use this website, various personal data are collected. Personal data is data with which you can be personally identified. This privacy policy explains what data we collect and what we use it for. It also explains how and for what purpose this happens.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          We point out that data transmission on the Internet (e.g., when communicating by email) can have security gaps. Complete protection of data against access by third parties is not possible.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Information on the Responsible Party</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          The responsible party for data processing on this website is:
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-2">
          <strong>Pascal Krason</strong><br />
          Schubertstr. 24<br />
          58762 Altena<br />
          Germany
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          The responsible party is the natural or legal person who alone or jointly with others decides on the purposes and means of processing personal data (e.g., names, email addresses, etc.).
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Storage Period</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Unless a more specific storage period has been specified within this privacy policy, your personal data will remain with us until the purpose for data processing no longer applies. If you assert a legitimate request for deletion or revoke consent for data processing, your data will be deleted, unless we have other legally permissible reasons for storing your personal data (e.g., tax or commercial retention periods); in the latter case, deletion will take place after these reasons no longer apply.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Note on Data Transfer to the USA and Other Third Countries</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          We use tools from companies based in the USA or other third countries that are not secure under data protection law. If these tools are active, your personal data may be transferred to these third countries and processed there. We point out that no level of data protection comparable to that in the EU can be guaranteed in these countries. For example, US companies are obliged to hand over personal data to security authorities without you as the data subject being able to take legal action against this. It cannot therefore be ruled out that US authorities (e.g., intelligence services) process, evaluate and permanently store your data located on US servers for monitoring purposes. We have no influence on these processing activities.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          <strong>Note:</strong> This website is hosted on GitHub Pages, a service of GitHub, Inc. based in the USA. When you visit this website, data is automatically transmitted to GitHub. For more information, please refer to the "Hosting" section.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">4. Data Collection on This Website</h2>
        
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Server Log Files</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          The provider of the pages automatically collects and stores information in so-called server log files, which your browser automatically transmits to us. These are:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-stone-700 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
          <li>Browser type and browser version</li>
          <li>Operating system used</li>
          <li>Referrer URL</li>
          <li>Hostname of the accessing computer</li>
          <li>Time of the server request</li>
          <li>IP address</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          This data is not merged with other data sources. The collection of this data is based on Art. 6 para. 1 lit. f GDPR. The website operator has a legitimate interest in the technically error-free presentation and optimization of his website - for this purpose, the server log files must be collected.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          <strong>Note:</strong> The server log files are created and stored by GitHub. We do not have direct access to this data. For more information, please refer to GitHub's privacy policy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">5. Web Analytics</h2>

        {/* The id must not be "clarity": an element id becomes a window
            property, `window.clarity` would then resolve to this heading, and
            the tracker's own queue shim (`window.clarity = window.clarity || fn`)
            would keep the element instead of installing itself — Clarity then
            throws and never starts, on this page only. */}
        <h3 id="microsoft-clarity" class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Microsoft Clarity</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          This website uses Microsoft Clarity, a web analytics service provided by Microsoft Corporation, One Microsoft Way, Redmond, WA 98052, USA ("Microsoft").
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          We use Clarity to understand how this website is actually used: which sections are read, where visitors click and scroll, and where a page is confusing. To do this, Clarity records your interactions with the site — mouse movement, clicks, scrolling, keyboard interaction, window and screen size, browser and operating system — together with the content of the pages you view. Microsoft aggregates these recordings into heatmaps and into individual session recordings that we can replay. Clarity does not ask you for your name, email address, or any other direct identifier, and we do not use it to identify you personally.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Legal Basis</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Clarity is loaded <strong>only after you have actively consented</strong> to it in our consent banner. Until you consent, no Clarity script is loaded, no connection to a Microsoft server is made, and no cookie is set. The legal basis for storing information on, and reading information from, your device is § 25 para. 1 TDDDG; the legal basis for the subsequent processing of your personal data is your consent under Art. 6 para. 1 lit. a GDPR. You may withdraw your consent at any time with effect for the future — see "Withdrawing Your Consent" below.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Declining has no consequences for you: the website works identically either way, and nothing else on this site depends on your choice.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Cookies Set by Clarity</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Once you have consented, Clarity stores the following cookies for the domain pkrason.de. Both contain randomly generated identifiers only.
        </p>
        <ul class="text-stone-700 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
          <li><code>_clck</code> — stores a randomly generated Clarity user ID so that repeat visits from the same browser can be recognised. Lifetime: <strong>365 days</strong>.</li>
          <li><code>_clsk</code> — links the pages you view during one visit into a single session recording. Lifetime: <strong>1 day</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          We have configured Clarity so that it does not grant Microsoft's advertising storage. It therefore does not set advertising cookies and does not link your visit to a Microsoft advertising profile. If you decline, or withdraw your consent, neither cookie is stored and any existing copy is deleted.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          Independently of your choice, we store your consent decision itself in your browser's local storage under the key <code>pk-consent-analytics</code>, so that we do not have to ask you again on every page. This entry contains only the value <code>granted</code> or <code>denied</code>, is never transmitted anywhere, and is strictly necessary to provide the function you requested (§ 25 para. 2 no. 2 TDDDG). You can remove it at any time by clearing this site's data in your browser.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Data Transfer to the USA</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          If you consent, the recorded data is transmitted to servers operated by Microsoft under the domain <code>clarity.ms</code> and may be processed in the United States. Microsoft Corporation is based in the USA, which is a third country within the meaning of the GDPR. Please also read the general note in the section "Note on Data Transfer to the USA and Other Third Countries" above.
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          For details on how Microsoft processes this data, please refer to Microsoft's privacy statement: <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/privacy/privacystatement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 hover:text-orange-700 underline">https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/privacy/privacystatement</a>
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-3 mt-6">Withdrawing Your Consent</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          You can withdraw your consent to Microsoft Clarity at any time, with effect for the future. Use the button below: it deletes the Clarity cookies, stops any further data from being sent, and reloads this page so that Clarity is not loaded again.
        </p>
        <button
          type="button"
          id="clarity-consent-revoke"
          class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 min-h-[44px] bg-stone-900 text-white font-medium rounded-lg hover:bg-stone-800 transition-colors"
        >
          Withdraw consent to Microsoft Clarity
        </button>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4 mt-4" id="clarity-consent-state" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          You can also delete the cookies at any time in your browser settings, or prevent them from being stored in the first place. Withdrawing your consent does not affect the lawfulness of the processing that took place before you withdrew it (Art. 7 para. 3 GDPR).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">6. Your Rights</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          You have the following rights:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-stone-700 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
          <li><strong>Right of access</strong> according to Art. 15 GDPR regarding your personal data processed by us</li>
          <li><strong>Right to rectification</strong> according to Art. 16 GDPR of incorrect data</li>
          <li><strong>Right to erasure</strong> according to Art. 17 GDPR of data stored by us</li>
          <li><strong>Right to restriction of processing</strong> according to Art. 18 GDPR</li>
          <li><strong>Right to data portability</strong> according to Art. 20 GDPR</li>
          <li><strong>Right to object</strong> according to Art. 21 GDPR</li>
          <li><strong>Revocation</strong> of your once given consent according to Art. 7 para. 3 GDPR</li>
          <li><strong>Right to lodge a complaint</strong> with a supervisory authority according to Art. 77 GDPR</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          If you have questions about these rights or wish to exercise them, you can contact us at any time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-4">7. Contact</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-4">
          If you have questions about data protection, you can contact us at any time:
        </p>
        <p class="text-stone-700 mb-2">
          <strong>Pascal Krason</strong><br />
          Schubertstr. 24<br />
          58762 Altena<br />
          Germany
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<script>
  import {
    denyConsent,
    isClarityLoaded,
    onDecisionChange,
    readDecision,
    GRANTED,
  } from '../scripts/consent.js';

  const STATE_TEXT = {
    granted: 'Microsoft Clarity is currently active because you allowed it.',
    denied:
      'Microsoft Clarity is switched off. No data is being sent and the Clarity cookies have been deleted.',
    // localStorage can be cleared at any time, so "no decision" needs its own
    // wording — showing "active" or nothing at all would misinform.
    none: 'You have not been asked yet, or you cleared your choice. Microsoft Clarity is switched off.',
  };

  const stateLine = document.getElementById('clarity-consent-state');
  const render = (decision) => {
    if (stateLine) stateLine.textContent = STATE_TEXT[decision ?? 'none'];
  };
  render(readDecision());
  onDecisionChange(render);

  document.getElementById('clarity-consent-revoke')?.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    // Reload only when Clarity is actually running: the library restarts itself
    // in lean mode on a denied consent instead of shutting down, and its script
    // tag cannot be unloaded, so one more upload fires after the opt-out. The
    // reload is what makes "stops any further data from being sent" true. The
    // stored decision is read rather than only `isClarityLoaded()` because the
    // order of the two hoisted module scripts on this page is not guaranteed.
    const wasActive = readDecision() === GRANTED || isClarityLoaded();
    await denyConsent();
    if (wasActive) window.location.reload();
  });
</script>

<style>
  @reference "../styles/global.css";

  :global(.prose) {
    @apply text-stone-700;
  }
  
  :global(.prose h2) {
    @apply text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4;
  }
  
  :global(.prose h3) {
    @apply text-stone-800 mt-6 mb-3;
  }
  
  :global(.prose p) {
    @apply mb-4 leading-relaxed;
  }
  
  :global(.prose a) {
    @apply text-orange-600 hover:text-orange-700 underline;
  }
  
  :global(.prose code) {
    @apply bg-stone-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-sm;
  }
  
  :global(.prose ul, .prose ol) {
    @apply mb-4 ml-6;
  }
  
  :global(.prose li) {
    @apply mb-2;
  }
</style>
